Readiness Probe Demo

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STEP 01 — Create a Pod with a readiness probe

- We'll use NGINX and check whether its HTTP endpoint responds successfully.
- The kubelet running on the node where the Pod is scheduled performs the probe.
- readiness-probe.yaml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: readiness-demo
  labels:
    app: readiness-demo
spec:
  containers:
    - name: nginx
      image: nginx:alpine
      ports:
        - containerPort: 80
      readinessProbe:
        httpGet:
          path: /
          port: 80
        initialDelaySeconds: 5
        periodSeconds: 5


# kubectl apply -f readiness-probe.yaml
pod/readiness-demo created


# kubectl get pod readiness-demo
NAME             READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
readiness-demo   1/1     Running   0          43s


What did we configure?
  - httpGet: Kubernetes sends an HTTP request to / on port 80.
  - initialDelaySeconds: 5: This tells kubelet to wait 5 seconds after the container starts before performing the first probe.
  - periodSeconds: 5: Kubelet performs an HTTP GET request to / on port 80 approximately every 5 seconds to check whether NGINX is ready to serve traffic.
If the endpoint responds successfully with an HTTP status between 200 and 399, the check succeeds.

Example timeline
0 sec  -  NGINX container starts
5 sec  -  First readiness probe
10 sec -  Second readiness probe

- if the readiness probe fails, Kubernetes marks the Pod as not ready. It does not restart the container.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

We'll deliberately break the readiness probe and observe whether Kubernetes restarts the container.

Step 1 — Break the probe

- Currently, your probe checks path: /, which NGINX serves successfully.
- Keep everything else unchanged. NGINX doesn't have a /health page by default, so it should return HTTP 404, causing the readiness probe to fail.

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: readiness-break-demo
  labels:
    app: readiness-break-demo
spec:
  containers:
    - name: nginx
      image: nginx:alpine
      ports:
        - containerPort: 80
      readinessProbe:
        httpGet:
          path: /health
          port: 80
        initialDelaySeconds: 5
        periodSeconds: 5


# kubectl apply -f readiness-probe.yaml
pod/readiness-break-demo created


